-- title:
--   Invariant chart element outside every Igusa valuation ring, q=3
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $A_0$ denotes the contraction `A.comap (algebraMap ↥k₀ (AlgebraicClosure ℚ))` of the valuation subring $A$ to $k_0$, a *level automorphism at* $\gamma^{-1}$ means an automorphism $\tau$ of $K_\ell/k_0$ satisfying [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.IsLevelAutAt ↥k₀ q ξ q (q ^ 2 * M') H₁ γ⁻¹ Kℓ τ`](def/ModularCurve_FullLevelLevelAutAt.html#L29), i.e. $\tau$ transforms every quotient of integral $q$-expansions of modular forms of level $\Gamma_{H_1}(q^2M')$ by the slash action of `conjElemN q γ⁻¹`, normalised through any embedding $k_0 \hookrightarrow \mathbb{C}$ sending $\xi$ to $e^{2\pi i/q}$, and the *chart algebra* is `chartAlgFin A₀ Kℓ jℓ`, the $A_0$-subalgebra of elements of $K_\ell$ integral over $A_0[j_\ell]$.
--
--   *Arithmetic data.* A prime $q$ with $q = 3$; a nonzero $M'$ with $q \nmid M'$ (`hqM'`); a prime $\ell_g$ with $\ell_g \equiv 11 \pmod{12}$ and $\ell_g \mid M'$; a valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with `A.LiesOverPrime q`, that is $q$ a non-unit of $A$ (`hA`); a finite set $W$ of places of `modularFunctionFieldC (ResidueField A) M'` over the residue field of $A$, which by `hW` is exactly the set `ssPlaces q M' (ResidueField A)` of supersingular places (rational, affine geometric, with $j$-evaluation in `ssJSet q`), together with a distinguished member $s \in W$; the inclusion `hle` of `modularFunctionFieldBar M'` in `fieldBar q M'`; and a constant reduction $R_0$ of $A$ from `modularFunctionFieldBar M'` to `modularFunctionFieldC (ResidueField A) M'`, whose residue map is compatible with coefficientwise reduction of Laurent series over $A$ (`hR₀`).
--
--   *The base field and its valuation.* An intermediate field $k_0$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q}$, an element $\pi_0 \in k_0$ lying in $A$ (`hπ`), and the hypotheses `hdvr`, `hunif`, `hhens`, `hres` asserting that $A_0$ is a discrete valuation ring, henselian, with maximal ideal generated by $\pi_0$ and algebraically closed residue field; `hκ` asserts that every element of $A$ is congruent modulo the maximal ideal of $A$ to an element of $k_0 \cap A$. Auxiliary constants: a prime $\ell$ with $\ell \ge 3$, $\ell \ne q$, $\ell \nmid M'$ (`hℓ`, `hℓ3`, `hℓq`, `hℓM'`) and $\zeta_0 \in k_0$ a primitive $q\ell$-th root of unity; $\varpi_t \in k_0$ lying in $A$ with $\varpi_t^{q^2-1} = q\,u$ for a unit $u$ of $A$ (`hϖtA`, `hϖt`), and $\varpi_t' \in A_0$ equal to $\varpi_t$ (`hϖt'`); $\xi \in k_0$ a primitive $q$-th root of unity (`hξ`); an intermediate field $K_b$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/k_0$ with $K_b = \bot$ (`hKb`), a valuation subring $A_b$ of $K_b$ cut out by $A$ (`hAb`) and $\varpi_b \ne 0$ generating its maximal ideal (`hϖb`, `hϖb0`); and the tameness hypothesis `htame`, that $\ell_g - 1$ is a unit of $A_0$.
--
--   *The function field and the point $y$.* The subgroup $H_1$ of $(\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^\times$ is the intersection of `levelH q M'` with the kernel of reduction to $(\mathbb{Z}/\ell_g)^\times$ (`hH₁`), and $K_\ell$ is the intermediate field `laurentBaseChange ↥k₀ (xHFunctionField (q ^ 2 * M') H₁)` of `LaurentSeries ↥k₀` over $k_0$ (`hKℓ`), an $A_0$-algebra compatibly with $k_0$; $j_\ell \ne 0$ is the image `coeffEmb ↥k₀ jq` of the $j$-expansion (`hjℓ`). Further, $y$ is a maximal ideal of the chart algebra (`hy`) containing $\pi_0$ (`hϖy`); `hss` asserts that for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $q$ and every ring homomorphism $\varphi$ from the chart algebra to $\Omega$ with kernel $y$, the value $\varphi(\mathrm{jChartFin} \ldots)$ lies in `ssJSet q Ω`; `hjK` puts `jqNModC ↥k₀ q` in $K_\ell$, `hjC` puts it in the chart algebra, and `ha₀y` asserts that it is congruent to the constant $a_0 \in A_0$ modulo $y$. The hypothesis `hover` states that $y$ lies over $s$: for every $g$ in the full modular function field of level $M'$ whose base change lies in the integers of $R_0$, which is regular at every place where $j$ is regular and whose $R_0$-residue lies in the valuation subring of $s$, and which lies in $K_\ell$ and in the chart algebra, and for every $c \in k_0 \cap A$ whose residue in the residue field of $A$ is the value at $s$ of that $R_0$-residue, the difference $g - c$ lies in $y$. The hypothesis `hOrbit` states that every maximal ideal $y'$ of the chart algebra containing $\pi_0$ and satisfying the same over-$s$ condition is obtained from $y$ by a level automorphism: there are $\gamma \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$ and a level automorphism $\tau$ at $\gamma^{-1}$ preserving the chart algebra and carrying $y$ to $y'$.
--
--   *The semistable chart data.* A subalgebra $B_t$ of $K_\ell$ over $A_0$, a valuation subring $W_t$ of $K_\ell$ with $B_t \subseteq W_t$ (`hBW`), and the hypothesis `hS1` (fourteen clauses, summarised here): the chart algebra is contained in $B_t$ and $K_\ell$ is its field of fractions; $B_t$ is formally smooth and of finite presentation over $A_0$ and $B_t/(\pi_0)$ has Krull dimension at most one; $W_t$ is a discrete valuation ring with $W_t \cap k_0 = A_0$, maximal ideal generated by $\pi_0$, it is the localisation of $B_t$ at that maximal ideal, and $y$ is the contraction of its maximal ideal to the chart algebra; for every $(\mathbb{F}_{q^2})$-algebra structure on the residue field of $A_0$ there is a surjection $\rho$ from $B_t$ onto [`DrinfeldCurve.CoordRing q (ResidueField A₀)`](def/DrinfeldCurve_CoordRing.html#L21) with kernel the contraction of the maximal ideal of $W_t$, compatible with constants, and equivariant for level automorphisms at $\gamma^{-1}$ with $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ preserving $W_t$ through an element $(\mathrm{redQ}\, q\, γ, c)$ of [`DrinfeldCurve.hSubgroup q`](def/DrinfeldCurve_CoordRing.html#L276) acting by [`DrinfeldCurve.hAction`](def/DrinfeldCurve_CoordRing.html#L325), with $c \ne 1$ for non-trivial $\tau$ coming from $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)$ and with the pair $(1,c)$ realised by some further $\gamma' \in \Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$; $B_t$ is stable under all level automorphisms at $\gamma^{-1}$ with $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$; every prime of $B_t$ containing $\pi_0$ is the image of the maximal ideal of $W_t$ under such an automorphism with $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$; and an element of $B_t$ whose images under all these automorphisms lie in the maximal ideal of $W_t$ is divisible by $\pi_0$.
--
--   *Deck data and the crossing model.* An integer $n$ with $1 \le n$ and $n \mid q+1$ (`hn1`, `hnq`), equal to twice `placeWidthChar q M' s` (`hn`); an element $\gamma_0 \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$ (`hγ₀Γ`, `hγ₀Γ0`) and a level automorphism $\tau_0$ at $\gamma_0^{-1}$ preserving $W_t$ (`hτ₀`, `hτ₀W`); an integer $m \ge 1$ with $\pi_0^m = \varpi_t'$ times a unit of $A_0$ (`hm1`, `hm`); an element $\zeta_c$ of the adic completion of $A_0$ with $\zeta_c^n = 1$ and $\zeta_c^k - 1$ a unit for $0 < k < n$ (`hζc`, `hζcprim`); and a finite set `ends` of subrings of $K_\ell$. The hypothesis `hR7` (twelve clauses, summarised here) asserts: $\tau_0^n$ is the identity on $B_t$ and no smaller positive power is, and every level automorphism at $\gamma^{-1}$ with $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$ preserving $W_t$ agrees on $B_t$ with some $\tau_0^k$, $k < n$; `ends` has exactly $q+1$ elements, is permuted by level automorphisms with $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ preserving $W_t$ and transitively so, each end is preserved by $\tau_0$, and distinct ends are separated by an element which is a unit in one and not in the other; there is a common finite-type $A_0$-subalgebra $B_c$ of $K_\ell$, stable under the level automorphisms with $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$, contained in every end and with each end a localisation of it; carrying an end into $W_t$ forces $W_t$ to be preserved; for each end $O$ there is a long clause asserting that $O \subseteq W_t$, that $O$ is local and noetherian, contains the chart algebra, satisfies $O \cap k_0 = A_0$ and has residue field generated by the constants, that its adic completion is identified by an isomorphism $\iota$ with the crossing model `UVCrossingModel` over the completion of $A_0$ with parameter $\pi_0^m$, matching constants and sending two elements $c_x, c_y$ with $c_xc_y = \pi_0^m u$ to unit multiples of the coordinates $U$ and $V$, with $c_y$ in and $c_x$ outside the maximal ideal of $W_t$, that `jqNModC ↥k₀ q` $-\,a_0$ corresponds to a unit times $V^e$ for some $e \ge 1$, that $\tau_0$ is transported by $\iota$ to an automorphism $\theta_0$ scaling $U$ by $\zeta_c$ and $V$ by its inverse modulo the square of the maximal ideal, and furthermore the existence of a discrete valuation subring $W_x$ of $K_\ell$ with constants $A_0$, containing $O$ and the chart algebra, uniformised by $\pi_0$, in which $j$ and a further element are residually transcendental over $A_0$, whose maximal ideal cuts out exactly $y$ on the chart algebra in the stated one-sided senses, which separates $O$ from the other ends and is not reached from another end by any level automorphism with $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$, and the existence of a finite-type $A_0$-subalgebra $B_x \subseteq B_t \cap O$, stable under those automorphisms, whose elements have no poles away from the poles of $j_\ell$, with $O$ the localisation of $B_x$ and containing an element detecting valuation subrings in which $j_\ell$ is residually transcendental; finally, every valuation subring of $K_\ell$ with constants $A_0$ containing the chart algebra and with $y$ in its maximal ideal either contains $B_t$ or dominates one of the ends.
--
--   *The two pole hypotheses.* `hR9` asserts the existence of $b \in B_t$ lying in no valuation subring $V$ of $K_\ell$ with $V \cap k_0 = A_0$, with $\pi_0$ in the maximal ideal of $V$, and such that no monic polynomial over $A_0$ evaluated at $j_\ell$ lies in the maximal ideal of $V$. `hR10` asserts that for every maximal ideal $y''$ of the chart algebra containing $\pi_0$ which is not the image of $y$ under any level automorphism at $\gamma^{-1}$ with $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$, there is $b \in B_t$ lying, for every such automorphism $\tau$, in no valuation subring $V$ of $K_\ell$ containing the chart algebra and whose maximal ideal contains the $\tau$-transport of $y''$.
--
--   *The descent block.* $G$ is the subgroup of $\mathrm{Aut}(K_\ell/k_0)$ generated by all level automorphisms at $\gamma^{-1}$ with $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$ (`hG`), $K_0$ is the fixed field of $G$ (`hK₀`), $B_0$ is the subalgebra of $G$-invariant elements of $B_t$ (`hB₀`), and $W_0'$ is the valuation subring of $K_0$ induced by $W_t$ (`hW₀'`).
--
--   *Conclusion.* There exists $b \in K_0$ whose image in $K_\ell$ lies in $B_0$ such that for every valuation subring $V$ of $K_0$ with the three properties:
--
--   (i) for all $x \in k_0$, $x \in A$ if and only if the image of $x$ in $K_0$ lies in $V$;
--
--   (ii) if the image of $\pi_0$ lies in $V$, then it lies in the maximal ideal of $V$;
--
--   (iii) whenever `coeffEmb ↥k₀ jq` lies in $K_\ell$, lies in $K_0$, and its class $j^\sharp$ lies in $V$, no monic polynomial $p \in k_0[X]$ all of whose coefficients lie in $A$ has $p(j^\sharp)$ in the maximal ideal of $V$;
--
--   it follows that $b \notin V$. Thus $b$ is a $G$-invariant element of $B_t$, viewed in the fixed field $K_0$, which has a pole at every valuation of $K_0$ whose constants are $A_0$, which is centred at $\pi_0$ and at which $j$ is residually transcendental over the constants.
--
--   This is the descent to the fixed field $K_0 = K_\ell^{G}$ of the pole hypothesis `hR9` of the rigid-chart package: it produces a $G$-invariant function in $B_0$ with a pole at every Igusa-type valuation of $K_0$, which is what pins down the affine chart downstairs. It is stated in the $q = 3$, $\Gamma_1(\ell_g)$-guard frame and is used by the small-$q$ level-descent theorem [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.Diamond.exists_supersingularDVR_affineChart_poles_moduliHasse_commonChart_nodes_igusaSep_deckSep_linkedScalars_linkedInertia_of_rigidChart_of_eq_three_of_dvd`](thm.html#ModularCurve.FullLevel.Diamond.exists_supersingularDVR_affineChart_poles_moduliHasse_commonChart_nodes_igusaSep_deckSep_linkedScalars_linkedInertia_of_rigidChart_of_eq_three_of_dvd) in the analysis of the semistable model of the modular curve at a supersingular point in characteristic $q$.
